3. Build the masthead — the newspaper's identity
This is the top strip, roughly the first 14–15% of the page.
Add a small text box at the top left: INTERNATIONAL EDITION.
Add a centered line: TODAY'S HEADLINES. TOMORROW'S WORLD.
At the top right, write 13 OCTOBER 2026 | VOL. 1, ISSUE 35 if you are using today's date and your own issue numbering.
Add the huge title THE ORBITAL TIMES in Bodoni Moda Black or Playfair Display ExtraBold. Make it almost the full width of the page.
For the globe inside the title's “O”, use a small circular Earth photograph. In Canva, add an Earth photo, crop it into a circle using Frames, then position it over the “O” if the font and spacing allow it. Otherwise, put the globe between “THE” and “ORBITAL” as a custom logo.
Under the masthead, add the subtitle:
A GLOBAL PERSPECTIVE ON SPACE, SECURITY AND A SHARED TOMORROW
7. Add a thin dark line, then a navigation strip:
SPACE SECURITY | TECHNOLOGY | GEOPOLITICS | ECONOMY | ENVIRONMENT | SCIENCE | THE FUTURE
Canva search terms: thin horizontal line, vintage newspaper divider, globe Earth circle, editorial newspaper header.
Keep the masthead background cream, not navy. That contrast is part of what makes the page look like a printed newspaper.
4. Create the giant space hero
This is the most visually important part of the page. It should occupy roughly one-fifth of the whole design.
Add a wide rectangular photo frame underneath the navigation strip.
Search for a real Earth-at-night or Earth-horizon image. Choose one with a bright horizon on the right and darker space on the left, so the headline remains readable.
Place the photo in the frame and crop it to fill the full width.
Add a dark navy rectangle over the left 60–70% of the image. Set its transparency to around 20–35% if your Canva controls allow it. The aim is to darken the photograph without hiding it.
Add a small red rectangle at the upper left with white text: SPECIAL REPORT.
Add the headline in three separate text boxes to control the line breaks:
THE NEXT BATTLEFIELD IS ABOVE US
Use Anton or League Spartan in white; make IS ABOVE US icy blue (#75D9F2). Align all three lines to the left. 7. Under the headline, add:
Military satellites, global infrastructure and a new era of competition in space.
8. At the far right, add a narrow quote box with a short line such as:
“Space is no longer a distant frontier.”
Treat this as your own editorial wording, not as a quotation from a named official.
Important: don't try to find one perfect photograph that already contains Earth, satellites, headline text and lighting. Build the scene from separate elements so it stays editable and looks less like an AI poster.
5. Add the statistics strip
Directly beneath the hero image, add a dark navy rectangle across the page.
Divide it into four or five cells using thin vertical lines. In each cell, place a small icon, a large icy-blue figure or phrase, and a short white explanation.
Suggested structure:
30,000+ — Active satellites in orbit
SPACE-BASED SERVICES — Everyday systems depend on satellites
GROWING ACTIVITY — Military and commercial interest in space
ORBITAL DEBRIS — Tracked fragments and objects pose collision risks
Use icons such as a globe, bar chart, shield, satellite and clock.
Don't copy all the numbers in the visual reference blindly. The figures shown in a mockup are not automatically verified facts. Confirm each number against a dated source before printing it, or replace it with a descriptive phrase. The same applies to the 60% figure and the debris count.
Canva Elements searches:
satellite line icon
globe outline icon
shield outline icon
bar chart line icon
vertical divider
Keep the numbers large and the descriptions short; the strip should read like a newspaper data bar, not a row of paragraphs.
6. Build the main news section
This section begins immediately below the statistics strip. Use three columns: a wider left column, a central infographic column, and a narrower right column.
Left column — Breaking news
Add a red label with white bold text: BREAKING NEWS.
Add the headline in Libre Baskerville Bold:
Russian satellite manoeuvres raise concerns over commercial networks
Put the article in a separate text box underneath, aligned left.
Use a readable font size; at A3 size, don't shrink the article until it is unreadable just to fit every word.
At the bottom, add a small satellite photo and a pull quote in a navy box.
Finish with a small source line: Source: Reuters, 9 October 2026.
Keep the story factual and clearly distinguish officials' concerns from proven events.

Centre column, upper half — Why satellites matter
Add a navy heading bar: WHY SATELLITES MATTER ON EARTH.
Under it, make four equal cards with a photo at the top and a caption below.
Use four photographs: a communications dish, an aircraft or navigation visual, a hurricane seen from space, and a satellite dish or secure communications installation.
Add a small circular icon overlapping the bottom edge of each image.
Card titles and captions:
COMMUNICATIONS — Internet, phone links and emergency connectivity.
NAVIGATION — GPS, aviation, shipping and precise timing.
EARTH OBSERVATION — Weather, climate, disasters and agriculture.
NATIONAL SECURITY — Defence, intelligence and secure communications.

Right column — The new space race
Add a navy panel with white title: THE NEW SPACE RACE.
Place a real rocket-launch photograph behind or beside the text.
Create a vertical timeline using a thin white line, three small circles and the years 2024, 2025 and 2026.
Keep each entry to one or two lines, and use only developments you can verify.
At the bottom, add a separate panel titled THE QUESTION FOR TOMORROW, with the large question:
Who keeps space safe?
This column should be visually striking, but its text should remain easy to read.
For real images, start with the NASA Image and Video Library and check the individual image credits and usage notes. NASA imagery is generally usable for educational and informational purposes under its media guidelines, but third-party material and NASA insignia can have separate restrictions.
7. Draw the orbital diagram yourself — this is where you can stand out
Don't use a stock infographic here. Create your own editable diagram in Canva. It will look more intentional, and you can explain exactly how you made it to the judges.
How to construct it
Create a wide rectangle with a navy background (#071522).
Add a small starfield. Search tiny stars transparent or space stars overlay; reduce the transparency so the stars don't compete with the labels.
Search Earth planet PNG or use a real Earth photograph. Place it at the bottom centre, enlarge it, and crop it so only the upper half of the globe is visible.
Search Canva Elements for satellite line icon. Choose two simple, similar icons.
Recolour one satellite red and the other icy blue.
Search dotted curved line, curved arrow or arc line. Create two curved paths around Earth, one red and one blue.
Add labels beside the paths:
RUSSIAN SATELLITES — Reported manoeuvres
ICEYE SATELLITE — Commercial radar-imaging spacecraft
CLOSE APPROACH — Satellites operate near one another
COLLISION / ATTACK — A possible risk or hypothetical action, not a claim that an attack occurred
8. Add a tiny caption at the bottom: ILLUSTRATIVE DIAGRAM — NOT TO SCALE.
Use the red and blue lines to make the information understandable at a glance. Do not draw a path that implies an actual collision occurred; the reported event involved close manoeuvres, not a confirmed collision. Reuters reported the concerns raised by Germany's space commander about Russian satellites approaching a Finnish ICEYE satellite in May 2026.

Reuters
Canva searches: satellite line icon, Earth planet photo, curved dotted arrow, arc line, tiny stars, glowing circle.
8. Create the bottom-left global space infographic
This is the panel titled A CROWDED AND CONTESTED SPACE.
Make a large navy rectangle in the bottom-left section.
Add the title in white, with a thin cyan line beneath it.
Use a world map graphic as the left half of the panel. Search world map outline, world map dots or dark world map infographic.
Overlay several curved orbital lines around a small Earth image or globe. Use cyan, red and gold, but keep the map visible.
Add a small legend at the bottom with coloured dots and labels, such as Major space actors, Commercial satellite networks and Orbital-risk areas.
On the right, stack three facts with icons:
Satellite numbers
Low Earth orbit altitude
Risks from interference, collisions and debris
Use figures only after verifying their meaning, date and source. For example, don't label a number as “active satellites” if its source actually counts all tracked objects. A useful starting point for space-environment information is the.
To make the map look professional: keep the world map muted and use bright colours only for the paths and markers. That creates depth without making the panel chaotic.
9. Add the editorial and final question
Editorial panel
Use a cream background in the lower centre, separated from neighbouring panels by a thin navy line.
Add a small cyan label: EDITORIAL.
Main heading:
SPACE MUST NOT BECOME THE NEXT FRONTIER OF CONFLICT
Write three short paragraphs:
Explain why communication, navigation and Earth observation matter.
Discuss how close satellite manoeuvres and misunderstandings could create risks.
Argue for transparency, communication between space agencies and stronger international norms.
This must sound like your newspaper's reasoned opinion, rather than another straight news report.
The question for tomorrow
Make this a tall, narrow panel at the lower right. Use a navy-to-blue background or a real space photograph.
Add a small cyan header: THE QUESTION FOR TOMORROW.
Make the question large, in an elegant italic serif font:
Who keeps space safe?
Below it, add:
Can nations agree on clearer rules for military satellite manoeuvres and prevent harmful interference in orbit?
The question should be one of the last things the reader sees, so leave breathing room around it.
10. Find the right real photographs
Use these searches rather than AI-generated images. Search the phrases in Canva Photos or on the linked source websites, then download suitable images and upload them to Canva.
Main hero image
Search: Earth at night from space, Earth horizon sunrise, Earth from orbit city lights.
Choose a wide image with dark space on one side, and use it behind your headline.
Satellite photos
Search: satellite solar panels Earth, spacecraft orbit Earth, communications satellite.
Use one for the breaking-news pull quote and another for the hero if the crop works.
Communications card
Search: satellite dish ground station, satellite communications antenna.
Use a photo with a strong silhouette so the icon and title can sit beneath it.
Earth-observation card
Search: hurricane from satellite, cyclone satellite imagery, Earth observation satellite.
Choose a genuine weather image rather than a decorative space illustration.
Space-race timeline
Search: rocket launch night, rocket launch space, launch vehicle liftoff.
Use the image as a narrow background strip, with a dark overlay behind the timeline text.
For NASA imagery, credit NASA and check the individual image's usage notes; not every image on NASA's websites is necessarily free of third-party restrictions.
11. Exact finishing touches that make it look like a real newspaper
Add thin dividers between every major section. Use navy or muted grey rather than thick decorative borders.
Use a tiny uppercase label above each major heading: SPECIAL REPORT, BREAKING NEWS, GLOBAL PERSPECTIVE, EDITORIAL.
Keep body text black on cream; reserve white text for dark panels.
Make all four satellite cards exactly the same width and align their image tops.
Use consistent padding inside every panel.
Add small photo credits and a short source line to each reporting or data section.
Add a subtle paper-grain texture only if it doesn't reduce readability.
Group each completed panel in Canva so you can move it without disturbing all its parts.
Keep the key text inside the page margins; Canva's official guide to margins, rulers and guides explains how to enable them.

